{"name":"Sausalito Port","hex":"#5d6f85","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"port","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place on the coast at which ships can shelter, or dock to load and unload cargo or passengers.","A town or city containing such a place, a port city."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to port, the left-hand side of a vessel when facing the bow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/port"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sausalito, California","blocks":[{"text":"Sausalito is a city in Marin County, California, United States, located 1.5 miles southeast of Marin City, 8 miles (13 km) south-southeast of San Rafael, and about 4 miles (6 km) north of San Francisco from the Golden Gate Bridge."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Sausalito.jpg/330px-Sausalito.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sausalito%2C_California"},{"title":"Port","blocks":[{"text":"A port is a maritime facility comprising one or more wharves or loading areas, where ships load and discharge cargo and passengers. Although usually situated on a sea coast or estuary, ports can also be found far inland, such as Hamburg, Manchester and Duluth; these access the sea via rivers or canals. Because of their roles as ports of entry for immigrants as well as soldiers in wartime, many port cities have experienced dramatic multi-ethnic and multicultural changes throughout their histories."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Manhattan.jpg/330px-Manhattan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/William_Richardson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Wil